Original commit body from d03eb69ad92597bc8a19cd8e96c86ad888363a81:

tree-sitter-swift parses ``xs[0]`` and ``r.headers["age"]`` as a
``call_expression``; the only difference from ``g(1)`` is that the
``value_arguments`` open with ``[``. The call pass never looked at the
bracket, so every subscript read was a call: to a FIELD
(``Response.headers:field``), to a parameter (``xs``), or a RESOLVED
phantom call to an unrelated free function that shared the parameter's
name (``data``).

``_is_subscript`` reads the bracket. The edge pass emits no call for a
subscript, and the receiver-typing walker types no element from one
(nothing tracks element types; a wrong constructor guess is worse than
none). The tree is iterated flat, so a call nested in a subscript's
receiver (``make()[0]``) is still a call.

Red on dev: exactly the three filed edges on the item's two fixtures.
Green: none of them; ``f -> make`` from ``make()[0]`` kept.

The module-key axis registry cites two Swift emission sites by line;
the insertion moved them by fifteen lines, so the citations follow.

